==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|kanzelære}}, from {{inh|de|goh|canzilāri}} (ca. 900), borrowed from {{der|de|LL.|cancellārius}}, derived from {{der|de|la|cancellus}}. {{surface analysis|de|Kanzel|-er}}, but semantically pertaining more closely to {{m|de|Kanzlei}} (itself derived from the former). Compare {{cog|en|chancellor}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈkan(t)sləʁ/|[ˈkant͡s.lɐ]}}
* {{audio|de|De-Kanzler.ogg|Audio}}

===Noun===
{{de-noun|m|f=in}}

# [[chancellor]] {{gloss|title of a head of government}}
## {{lb|de|Germany|Austria}} {{short for|de|Bundeskanzler}}
## {{lb|de|historical}} {{short for|de|Reichskanzler}}
# [[chancellor]] {{gloss|kind of secretary}}
## {{lb|de|education}} head of the [[administration]] of a [[university]]
## {{lb|de|diplomacy}} head of the administration of an [[ambassy]] or [[consulate]]
## {{lb|de|historical}} head of a [[chancellory]]; chief [[secretary]] of a [[bishop]] or [[ruler]] 

